          "content": "At dawn, the city looked as if someone had rinsed it overnight. Rain clung to the windows of the bakery, and every streetlight wore a trembling halo. I walked without an umbrella, not because I had forgotten one, but because the weather seemed to be asking for company.\n\nAt the corner, a woman in a yellow coat was feeding crumbs to pigeons. They gathered around her boots like tiny, impatient commuters. She smiled at them with the grave attention people usually reserve for important guests, and for a moment the morning felt less like a beginning than a secret already underway.\n\nAcross the street, a bus sighed open and released a handful of passengers. One man carried a bouquet wrapped in newspaper; another held a paper cup carefully, as though it contained a small, sleeping animal. Nobody seemed in a hurry. The rain had softened the edges of things, even the usual sharpness of time.\n\nI stopped beneath the awning and listened. Water tapped the metal gutter in uneven rhythms, like a drummer practicing somewhere behind a wall. A window opened above me, and someone began playing piano. The notes were hesitant, then confident, then wrong again. I liked the mistakes best; they made the music sound inhabited.\n\nWhen the song ended, the rain was still falling, but the city had changed. Or perhaps I had. I bought a warm loaf, wished the baker good morning, and carried its bright, yeasty smell home through streets shining like polished stones again.",
